\section{Cross-Session Themes}
\label{ch:themes-command}

While individual sessions track themes for their kept papers, a
researcher working across many sessions needs a bird's-eye view.
The [[themes]] command group aggregates themes from all sessions,
answering two questions:
\begin{description}
\item[themes list] What themes exist and how many papers use each?
\item[themes show] Which papers belong to a particular theme?
\end{description}

These commands complement the per-session reports by providing a
cross-cutting view that helps identify the dominant research areas in a
review corpus.

<<themes command>>=
themes_app = typer.Typer(
    name="themes",
    help="Explore themes across review sessions.",
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
app.add_typer(themes_app, name="themes")


@themes_app.command("list")
def themes_list(
    output_format: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Option(
            "--format", "-f",
            help="Output format: table or json.",
            autocompletion=complete_display_format,
        ),
    ] = "table",
) -> None:
    """
    List all themes across review sessions.

    Shows each theme with the number of kept papers tagged with it.
    """
    from scholar.review import list_all_themes

    console = Console()
    themes = list_all_themes()

    if not themes:
        console.print(
            "[dim]No themes found. Tag papers with themes "
            "during review sessions.[/dim]"
        )
        return

    if output_format == "json":
        import json
        print(json.dumps(themes, indent=2))
    else:
        table = Table(
            title="Themes Across Sessions",
            box=box.HORIZONTALS,
        )
        table.add_column("Theme", style="cyan")
        table.add_column(
            "Papers", style="green", justify="right",
        )

        for theme, count in themes.items():
            table.add_row(theme, str(count))

