\subsection{BibTeX formatter}

For \LaTeX{} users, we generate BibTeX entries that can be added to a
[[.bib]] file.

The citation key is the first author's surname glued to the year.  We compute
it in a free function rather than inside the entry builder because the
provenance-stub formatter below needs the same key for its comment header
\emph{before} the entry itself is rendered --- sharing one function guarantees
the header and the entry never disagree.  A missing \emph{or blank} author
list falls back to [[unknown]] and a missing year to [[nd]], so a key always
exists: we take the surname from the split parts only when there is a part to
take, since a blank author string splits to nothing.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _bibtex_key(paper) -> str:
    """Return the citation key for a paper (first surname plus year)."""
    parts = paper.authors[0].split() if paper.authors else []
    first_author = parts[-1] if parts else "unknown"
    year = paper.year or "nd"
    return f"{first_author.lower()}{year}"
@

The entry builder returns the [[@article]] block as a string instead of
printing it line by line.  A returned string is what lets the provenance-stub
formatter wrap the very same entry in a comment block without re-implementing
any field formatting.

<<formatter classes>>=
class BibTeXFormatter:
    """Output results as BibTeX entries."""

    def format(self, results: list) -> None:
        """Output results as BibTeX."""
        for result in results:
            for paper in result.papers:
                print(self._format_paper(paper))
                print()

    def _format_paper(self, paper) -> str:
        """Return a single paper rendered as a BibTeX entry string."""
        lines = [
            f"@article{{{_bibtex_key(paper)},",
            f"  title = {{{paper.title}}},",
            f"  author = {{{' and '.join(paper.authors)}}},",
        ]
        if paper.year:
            lines.append(f"  year = {{{paper.year}}},")
        if paper.doi:
            lines.append(f"  doi = {{{paper.doi}}},")
        if paper.venue:
            lines.append(f"  journal = {{{paper.venue}}},")
        lines.append("}")
        return "\n".join(lines)
@

